<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning How We Take Care of Our Past – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning How We Take Care of Our Past</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write personal letters or emails to express opinions. Interactive: Engage in writing exchanges about future aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write emails to peers about the importance of preserving cultural heritage, sharing personal perspectives (e.g., "I have learned about the importance of preserving both architectural sites like Casco Viejo and traditional crafts like mola weaving.") Can participate in writing exchanges discussing the significance of Panama's historical landmarks (e.g., "I believe Portobelo should be preserved because it's part of our history.") Can compose letters to local officials, expressing opinions on cultural preservation efforts (e.g., "It's essential to protect our heritage sites for future generations.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of iconic Panamanian heritage sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo, Portobelo, Emberá village). Students are asked to quickly jot down 3-5 words or short phrases that come to mind for each image in their notebooks, focusing on what they remember about these places from previous lessons. They then briefly share one idea with a partner, identifying which site they believe is most relevant for preservation. This activates prior vocabulary like <i>heritage, sites, culture, traditional, preserve, important</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple opinion sentence on the board about one of the sites, for example: "I believe Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, should be protected." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'heritage' /ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/ and 'trade' /treɪd/, emphasizing the long /eɪ/ sound. The teacher also highlights the use of the relative clause "which is..." for adding information. The teacher then models how to express a strong opinion clearly and respectfully in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I write 'I believe Portobelo, where many historical events took place, is critical to our history,' am I stating a fact or an opinion?" (Expected answer: An opinion.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Casco Viejo, which attracts many tourists,' what does 'which' refer to?" (Expected answer: Casco Viejo.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a short model email (approx. 150-200 words) from a Panamanian student to a friend abroad, discussing the importance of preserving cultural heritage in Panama. The email incorporates relative clauses ("which," "where"), past simple vs. present perfect verbs (e.g., "The Panama Canal opened in 1914," "It has attracted many tourists"), future simple for predictions, and vocabulary such as <i>heritage, sites, culture, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, became, continue</i>. It also includes pragmatic functions like "Why is preserving historic landmarks like Casco Viejo... important?" and sociolinguistic expressions about discussing heritage respectfully ("I learned so much about the Emberá community...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand the main ideas and opinions expressed in written correspondence about cultural preservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the email individually and answer the following questions in writing:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the main purpose of the email? (Gist question)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">According to the email, which historical landmark is mentioned as a UNESCO World Heritage Site? (Specific information)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What opinion does the writer express about the future of cultural preservation efforts? (Identifying opinion)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice constructing sentences and paragraphs using relative clauses (which, where), past simple vs. present perfect, and future simple, along with core vocabulary (<i>heritage, history, sites, tourists, culture, restoration, weaving, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, became, transformed, continue, restore, play, relevant, historical, traditional, global, critical, cultural, significant, interesting, colonial</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences related to Panamanian heritage. Students complete the sentences by choosing the correct relative pronoun (which/where) or the appropriate verb tense (past simple, present perfect, future simple) from a given list, and adding relevant vocabulary words. Example: "The ancient city of Panama Viejo, _____ was destroyed by pirates, represents our rich _____. (which / heritage)" or "The Guna people _____ beautiful molas for centuries. (have woven / wove)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Paragraph Writing:</b> Students are given a topic sentence, e.g., "Preserving our cultural heritage is essential for Panama's future," and a set of prompts or keywords (e.g., <i>Casco Viejo, colonial architecture, attract tourists, income, identity, future generations, will protect</i>). They write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) expressing their opinion and supporting it with the prompts, ensuring they incorporate at least one relative clause and a mix of tenses. The teacher reminds them to consider the pronunciation of words like "important" /ɪmˈpɔː.tənt/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Peer Check for Accuracy:</b> Students exchange their written paragraphs from the previous activity with a partner. Using a simple checklist (e.g., "Did my partner use 'which' or 'where' correctly?", "Are the verbs in the correct tense?", "Are there at least 3 vocabulary words from our list?"), they review each other's writing for grammatical accuracy and vocabulary use. They provide constructive feedback to help correct errors.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "My Heritage, My Opinion" Email Exchange</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will write an email to a peer (either a classmate or an imagined friend from another country) sharing their personal perspectives on the importance of preserving a specific cultural heritage aspect in Panama. This could be an architectural site (like Casco Viejo), a traditional craft (like mola weaving), a historical event, or an indigenous culture (like the Emberá community). They must clearly express their opinion, using at least two relative clauses, correctly using past simple and present perfect tenses to describe its history or impact, and making at least one prediction using future simple about its preservation. They should integrate at least five vocabulary words from the recommended list. The email should also incorporate pragmatic functions like describing historical sites ("The Panama Canal opened in 1914, transforming global trade...") and sociolinguistic elements by discussing heritage respectfully ("I learned so much about the Emberá community...").</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual email writing (approx. 150-200 words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instructions:</b>
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Choose a specific Panamanian cultural heritage aspect you feel strongly about preserving.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write an email to a peer expressing your opinion on why it's important to preserve it.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Include:
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Your personal perspective (e.g., "I have learned about the importance of preserving...").</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two relative clauses (e.g., "Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO site," or "the museum where we learned...").</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples using past simple and present perfect tenses (e.g., "The site opened in 1914," "It has attracted many tourists.").</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One prediction using future simple (e.g., "We will continue to preserve...").</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least five vocabulary words from the lesson.</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus on expressing your ideas clearly and coherently, linking sentences logically to form a well-structured email.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Heritage, My Opinion" emails for teacher review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of opinion and supporting arguments (40%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of relative clauses (which/where), past simple, present perfect, and future simple (30%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (at least 5 words) (20%)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence and legibility of the email (10%)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by answering the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new thing I learned about writing opinions effectively in English today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which grammar point (relative clauses, tenses) did I find most challenging or most useful in my writing?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflections on the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How confident do I feel now about writing an email to express my opinion on cultural preservation? What specific part of my writing could I still improve?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How has learning to write about heritage helped me understand the importance of our past even better?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The controlled and semi-controlled writing practice activities worked well for reinforcing grammar and vocabulary before the main production task. Students demonstrated a good understanding of the theme and were able to formulate their opinions. Some students still struggled with consistently applying the correct past simple vs. present perfect tenses, and some relative clauses were slightly awkward. Adjustments for the next lesson could include a quick review of verb tense choices or providing more sentence frames for complex sentence structures if needed. The peer check was effective in highlighting common errors. The pronunciation practice of /eɪ/ and reduced vowels was integrated, but could be reinforced through a quick dictation exercise in the next warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize and present their written opinions about cultural preservation, potentially adapting their emails into a short presentation or a collaborative digital project, thus consolidating their understanding and productive skills in a more integrated way.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>